# Caddyfile for the swarm-relay reverse proxy (agents-tracker-vzl).
#
# Caddy terminates TLS with an automatic ACME certificate (Let's Encrypt by default) and
# proxies straight through to the relay's local listener. swarm-relay itself
# (internal/remote/relay/server.go) registers exactly one HTTP route -- mux.HandleFunc("/",
# s.handleHTTP) -- so every path funnels to the same handler and there is no sub-path to
# route around. It serves plain ws:// there and always will: Config.TLSMode is parsed by
# relay.LoadConfig but read by nothing that serves (docs/operations/relay-runbook.md
# section 0), so this file is the entire TLS story for the deployment.
#
# Caddy's reverse_proxy detects the "Connection: Upgrade" handshake and proxies WebSocket
# connections with NO extra directives -- unlike nginx, which needs an explicit
# Upgrade/Connection header pass-through block. Nothing else is required below.
#
# Replace relay.example.com with the DNS name pointed at this VPS (see
# docs/operations/relay-vps-deploy.md, "Point DNS"). Keep it SHORT: `swarm remote init`
# caps --relay-url at 39 characters (pairing.MaxRelayURLLen), because the URL is carried
# verbatim into the pairing QR, and "wss://" alone already spends 6 of those.
#
# The backend address must match "listen" in relay.config.example exactly.
#
# AMENDED BY ADR-016 (2026-08-15), landed with wave R2 (ADR-016:5): `reuse_private_keys` is now
# EXPERT-ONLY, not load-bearing for every deployment, because the DEFAULT relay TLS policy is
# `webpki` -- ordinary Web PKI hostname validation against this certificate, no pin configured or
# consulted at all -- and under that policy Caddy's own documented default ("a new key is created
# for every new certificate to mitigate pinning and reduce the scope of key compromise") is
# correct again; this directive buys a default `webpki` deployment nothing and may be dropped once
# no paired device is still inside the compatibility window (below). Keep this directive if you
# have chosen the expert `pinned_spki` policy for this relay (`--relay-tls-policy pinned_spki
# --relay-pin`), or while a paired device has not yet migrated off a `webpki` machine's
# compatibility pin (`--relay-pin-compat`, ADR-016 W9) -- in either case the paragraph below still
# applies:
#
# `reuse_private_keys` IS LOAD-BEARING TODAY AND IS NOT A PERFORMANCE SETTING.
# `swarm remote init --relay-pin` records the SPKI hash, the phone persists it
# (internal/phonecore/state.go, relay_spki_pin) and dials with
# PinnedSPKISHA256 forever after (mobile/relay.go). Without this directive, the first ACME
# renewal -- roughly 60 days after deployment, unattended -- presents a new key, the SPKI
# changes, and BOTH the phone and the machine start failing with ErrPinMismatch at once. The
# phone shows its RELAY_UNTRUSTED banner (ui/ConnectionUi.kt) -- "it is not presenting the
# identity your machine published when you paired" -- with no spinner, because this is not a
# link that comes back on its own. A routine renewal reads to the user as a security failure.
#
# Caddy documents this option as "against industry best practices" and "subject to removal in
# a future version". That is a real risk and the reason relay-vps-deploy.md carries a recovery
# procedure rather than treating the pin as permanent: if a Caddy upgrade ever drops it, the
# pin WILL change and the fix is to recompute it and re-pair, not to debug an interception
# that never happened.
relay.example.com {
	tls {
		reuse_private_keys
	}
	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:9440
}
